 The remainder when \(2001 \cdot 2002 \cdot 2003 \cdot 2004 \cdot 2005\) is divided by 19 can be found using modular arithmetic. First, we find the remainders of 2001, 2002, 2003, 2004, and 2005 when divided by 19. \[
2001 \equiv 2001 - 105 \cdot 19 \equiv 2001 - 2005 \equiv -4 \equiv 15 \pmod{19}
\] \[
2002 \equiv 2002 - 105 \cdot 19 \equiv 2002 - 2005 \equiv -3 \equiv 16 \pmod{19}
\] \[
2003 \equiv 2003 - 105 \cdot 19 \equiv 2003 - 2005 \equiv -2 \equiv 17 \pmod{19}
\] \[
2004 \equiv 2004 - 105 \cdot 19 \equiv 2004 - 2005 \equiv -1 \equiv 18 \pmod{19}
\] \[
2005 \equiv 2005 - 105 \cdot 19 \equiv 2005 - 2005 \equiv 0 \pmod{19}
\] Now, we need to find the remainder when \(15 \cdot 16 \cdot 17 \cdot 18 \cdot 0\) is divided by 19. Since one of the factors is 0, the entire product is 0. Thus, \[
2001 \cdot 2002 \cdot 2003 \cdot 2004 \cdot 2005 \equiv 0 \pmod{19}
\]
The answer is 0. </answer>

